e('That\'s the real definition of a pod: **a group of containers sharing a network namespace and a parent cgroup**.','For this native Linux example, shared namespaces and a parent cgroup help implement the pod\'s coordinated workload abstraction. They are not a complete cross-platform definition of a pod.','PodnotdefinedonlyLinuxprimitives.',[pods])
e('Istio\'s init container needs `CAP_NET_ADMIN` to write iptables rules into that shared namespace.','An Istio init-container interception setup needs network-administration privileges; Istio CNI can instead arrange interception outside that init container.','IstioCNIalternative.', ['https://istio.io/latest/docs/setup/additional-setup/cni/'])
e('a new process `setns()`s into the container\'s existing namespaces and cgroup.','a runtime helper configures namespace membership (including a fork for PID namespace entry), root, cgroup and process security before executing the command.','SetnsnotcgroupmembershiporPIDcaller.',[sn])
e('Take this container spec:','For the following container-level example, assume cgroup v2, CPU quota enforcement, a 100 ms quota period and no pod-level resource override:','Exactcgroupvaluesdependconfig.',[res,cm])
e('| memory request | (scheduler only) | — |','| memory request | scheduling/priority; optional protection | configuration dependent |','Memoryrequestnotonlyscheduler.',[res,qos])
e('If the node is idle, a container with a 250m request can still use several cores. If every container is busy, CPU is divided in proportion to weight.','A sufficiently parallel container may exceed its request when allowed by limits, affinity and ancestors. Eligible competing sibling cgroups receive relative weight-based service; this is not a flat global split of every container on the node.','Hierarchy/quotaconstraints.',[cg])
e('Only ratios between siblings matter, so neither formula changes what requests *mean*.','The curve preserves selected endpoints/defaults, not every ratio: actual weights and contention shares can change across implementations. Inspect cpu.weight on the deployed runtime instead of assuming a 2:1 request always becomes exactly a 2:1 weight.','Nonlinearcurvechangesratios.',[cv])
e('Once the quota is used up, every thread in the cgroup is descheduled until the next period starts.','Runnable fair-class tasks are throttled when the applicable budget is exhausted. Periods, runtime slices, burst configuration and ancestor quotas affect observed timing.','Notallthreadsschedulerclasses/predictableexact.',[cg])
e('This is the most common Kubernetes latency trap. Consider a 4-thread server with a 500m limit:','One possible latency cause is quota exhaustion. In an idealised case with four continuously running threads on four available CPUs, no burst credit and no tighter ancestor limit:','Unverifiedmostcommonandparallelpreconditions.',[cg])
e('even though the container\'s *average* CPU use is well under its limit.','even when its average over a longer, partly idle measurement interval is below the limit.','Sustainedexampleaveragesexactlimitnotbelow.',[cg])
e('A Python sketch of the arithmetic the kubelet and runc perform:','A simplified model of the ordinary quota/share conversion (integer millicores; excludes feature flags, pod budgets and special policies):','Codeexplicitnotfullruntimeimplementation.',[cm])
e('    quota = limit_m * PERIOD // 1000','    if limit_m <= 0:\n        raise ValueError("positive limit")\n    quota = max(1000,\n                limit_m * PERIOD // 1000)','Kernel/kubeletminimumquota1ms.',[cm])
e('    return max(2, request_m * 1024 // 1000)','    if request_m < 0:\n        raise ValueError("negative request")\n    return min(262144,\n        max(2, request_m * 1024 // 1000))','IncludeupperCPUsharesclamp.',[cm])
e('Memory cannot be throttled the way CPU can. A page in use can\'t simply be paused.','Memory allocation can be throttled/reclaimed, for example through memory.high; that differs from periodically pausing runnable CPU work.','Originalmemorycannotthrottlefalse.',[cg])
e('Kubernetes reports this as `OOMKilled`, exit code 137 (128 + SIGKILL\'s 9).','If the container\'s process is killed and the runtime identifies OOM, termination commonly appears as OOMKilled with exit code 137. A killed child need not terminate the entire container, and exit 137 alone does not prove OOM.','OOMchildvsinitand137notdiagnostic.',[res,cg])
e('So **CPU limits throttle; memory limits kill.**','As a useful shorthand, CPU quotas throttle and unrecoverable memory pressure can kill; allocation failures, reclaim and configuration create exceptions.','Qualifiedshortcut.',[cg])
e('its pages count against the pod\'s memory and are *not* reclaimable.','its pages are charged to the writing container\'s cgroup. They cannot be discarded like clean disk cache; swap availability/configuration can allow shmem pages to leave RAM.','tmpfsnotpinnedalways/unreclaimablewhenswap.',[res])
e('The MemoryQoS feature can additionally set `memory.min` and `memory.high`.','MemoryQoS can configure protection/throttling according to the Kubernetes version and kubelet settings; inspect the actual memory.min, memory.low and memory.high values.','CurrentMemoryQoSconfig/versiondependent.',[qos])
e('Kubernetes derives a QoS class from requests and limits:','For ordinary Linux pods using container-level CPU/memory resources, the following summary applies. Pod-level resource specifications and critical-pod exceptions require additional rules:','NewpodlevelQoSandcriticalscoreexceptions.',[qos,oq])
