e('**No memory limits** make a pod Burstable or BestEffort with nothing to stop a leak. It will be evicted, or will push neighbours into the OOM killer.','**No container memory limit** can allow growth until other constraints apply. Pod/ancestor limits, admission defaults and node policy may still constrain it; inspect the effective configuration rather than predicting a guaranteed failure.','Podlevelancestor/defaultsnotnothingstops.',[res])
e('**`hostPath`, `hostNetwork` and `hostPID`** remove the namespaces that made it a container.','**`hostPath`, `hostNetwork` and `hostPID`** expose selected host resources or share specific namespaces; they do not remove every namespace or security control.','HostPathnotnamespaceremoval.',[ps])
e('- A pod is a pause container holding shared net, IPC and UTS namespaces plus a pod cgroup; each container adds its own mount and PID namespaces.','- A typical native Linux pod sandbox anchors shared namespaces; host settings, process sharing and alternative runtimes change the details.','Crossplatformdefinitionfalse.',[pods])
e('- Memory limits become `memory.max`; exceeding it triggers the cgroup OOM killer (exit 137). QoS classes set `oom_score_adj` and eviction order.','- Memory limits drive accounting/reclaim/OOM behavior; exit 137 alone does not prove OOM. QoS influences OOM adjustments, while kubelet eviction uses its own pressure ranking.','Correctdiagnosticandordering.',[ev,cg])
e('- securityContext fields map one-to-one onto setuid, `no_new_privs`, capabilities, seccomp and user namespaces; Pod Security Admission enforces them; RuntimeClass swaps runc for gVisor or Kata.','- Security settings configure credentials and kernel restrictions. Admission checks selected policy rules; RuntimeClass selects a preconfigured supported runtime handler.','Notone-to-one/norRuntimeClassinstall.',[ps,rc])
